      "prism:url": "https://api.elsevier.com/content/abstract/scopus_id/51649189684",
      "dc:identifier": "SCOPUS_ID:51649189684",
      "eid": "2-s2.0-51649189684",
      "dc:title": "Concentration of work in open-cut mining of minerals",
      "dc:creator": "Kortelev O.V.",
      "prism:publicationName": "Soviet Mining Science",
      "prism:issn": "00385581",
      "prism:eIssn": "15738736",
      "prism:volume": "2",
      "prism:issueIdentifier": "5",
      "prism:pageRange": "522-526",
      "prism:coverDate": "1966-01-01",
      "prism:coverDisplayDate": "September 1966",
      "prism:doi": "10.1007/BF02497645",
      "dc:description": "1. The maximum load on the face is not necessarily the optimum value from the viewpoint of economic efficiency of the working system and the transport equipment. The optimum load should be determined separately in each case, taking into account the cost of 1 m<sup>3</sup> of overburden and the outlay. 2. For the conditions adopted the most economic load on the face will be the maximum possible bench height and lift width (for the given type of excavator), the minimum distance between the face and the interchange site, and working front length 0.5 km. 3. Combination of track relaying with excavator maintenance and performance of this work on the Sunday of a six-day working week greatly increases the load on the face and the cost of removing 1 m<sup>3</sup> of overburden, particularly in the case of small bench heights and short working fronts. 4. The method described can be used for establishing rational characteristics for the working system in rocks of different degrees of hardness and for different types of loading and transport equipment. © 1966 Consultants Bureau.",
